<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s09e05" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 5: Slope of a Line</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 5 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret the slope of a line
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Identify the slope of a line from its graph
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate the slope of a line from two given points on the line
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 5 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      Slope
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    In this episode, we will focus on graphs which are straight line. Straight lines have a slope which tell you how they are slanted. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Positive slope: Line goes up as you move from left to right
        </p>
        <figure>
        <caption>A line with a positive slope</caption>
        <image width="50%">
        <prefigure label="pf-positive-slope" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = x/2+1         
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Negative slope: Line goes down as you move from left to right
        </p>
        <figure>
        <caption>A line with a negative slope</caption>
        <image width="50%">
        <prefigure label="pf-negative-slope" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x/2+1         
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Big slope: Line is very steep!
        </p>
        
        <figure>
        <caption>A line with a steep slope</caption>
        <image width="50%">
        <prefigure label="pf-big-slope" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 5*x-1         
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Small slope: Line is not very steep
        </p>
        <figure>
        <caption>A line with a small slope</caption>
        <image width="50%">
        <prefigure label="pf-small-slope" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = x/10+1         
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-slope">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>slope</term> of a line tells you how they are slanted. The formula for slope is “rise over run”.
        <md>
          \text{slope}\,\, = \,\,\frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <image source="slope.png">
    <shortdescription>the graph of a line showing run as the horizontal change and rise as the vertical change for a line.</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <example>
    <p>
      A roof has a rise of 4 feet and a run of 12 feet. Find the slope of the roof. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The slope is the fraction
      <md>
        \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{4\,\,\text{feet}}{12\,\,\text{feet}} = \frac{1}{3}
      </md>
      $0
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      A wheelchair ramp has a rise of 4 inches and a run of 4 feet. Find the ramp’s slope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: First, we need all the measurements to have the same units. Let’s convert the run from feet to inches. 
      First, let’s look up the relationship between feet and inches, and convert that relationship to an equation in the form (desired)/(have).
      <md>
        1\,\,\text{foot} = 12\,\,\text{inches}
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{\text{desired}}{\text{have}} = \frac{12\,\,\text{inches}}{1\,\,\text{foot}} 
      </md>
      Now let’s use this fraction to convert our run from feet to inches:
      <md>
        4\,\,\text{feet}\times\frac{12\,\,\text{inches}}{1\,\,\text{foot}} = 48\,\,\text{inches}
      </md>
      Now, the slope is the fraction:
      <md>
        \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{4\,\,\text{inches}}{48\,\,\text{inches}} = \frac{1}{12}
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Find the slope of the line pictured below:
    </p>
    <image width="60%">
    <shortdescription>A graph of the line y = 2x</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex1a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 2*x        
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Solution: Mark any two easy-to-read points on the graph and measure the rise and the run between them.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
        <shortdescription>A graph of the line y = 2x with the points (0,0) and (1,2) marked</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex1b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 2*x        
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(0,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
          <point p="(1,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
          <line endpoints="(0,0),(1,0)" stroke="green"/>
            <line endpoints="(1,0),(1,2)" stroke="green"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        <label anchor="(0.5,0)" alignment="south">
        run
        </label>
        <label anchor ="(1,1)" alignment="south" rotate="90">
        rise
        </label>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The run is the horizontal distance between the two points. In the picture, the run is 1 unit. 
        </p>
        <p>
          The rise is the vertical distance between the two points. In the picture, the rise is 2 units. 
        </p>
        <p>
          So, the slope is
          <md>
            \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{2}{1} = 2
          </md>
          
        </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    If <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> and <m>(x_2,y_2)</m> are two different points on the graph of a line, the slope can be found using the formula:
    <md>
      \text{slope}\,\, = \frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}
    </md>
    The difference between the y-coordinates is the rise and the difference between the x-coordinates is the run.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      A line passes through the points (0,1) and (1,-3). Find the slope of the line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1: Plug the points <m>(x_1,y_1)=(0,1)</m> and <m>(x_2,y_2)=(1,-3)</m> into the formula for the slope of a line:
      <md>
        \text{slope}\,\,=\frac{(-3)-1}{1-0}
      </md>
      <md>
        =\frac{-4}{1} = -4
      </md>
    </p>
     <p>
      Solution 1: Plug the points <m>(x_1,y_1)=(1,-3)</m> and <m>(x_2,y_2)=(0,1)</m> into the formula for the slope of a line:
      <md>
        \text{slope}\,\,=\frac{1-(-3)}{0-1}
      </md>
      <md>
        =\frac{4}{-1} = -4
      </md>
      Notice that the slope is big and negative. This is because the line is steep, and it goes down as we follow it from left to right:
      <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of the line y = -4x + 1</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 1-4*x        
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s09e05">
    <title>Season 9, Episode 5 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
      <p>
        The ‘pitch’ of a building’s roof is the slope of the roof. Knowing the pitch is important in climates where there is heavy snowfall. 
        If the roof is too flat, the weight of the snow may cause it to collapse. What is the ‘pitch’ of a roof with a rise of 9 feet and a run of 18 feet?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
      <p>
        Have you ever thought about the sewage pipes going from your house to the street? They must slope down <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> 
        inch per foot in order to drain properly. What is the required slope?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.75in">
      <p>
        A local road has a grade of 6%. The grade of a road is its slope expressed as a percent. 
        Find the slope of the road as a fraction and then simplify. What rise and run would reflect this slope or grade?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      Find the slope from the following graphs:
          </p>
          <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
        <exercise workspace="0.5in">
          <image>
      <shortdescription>a graph of the line y = 5/4x-1</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 5/4*x-1        
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.5in">
          <image>
      <shortdescription>a graph of the line y = 5/4x-1</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex4" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -1/3*x+2       
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </exercise>
      </sidebyside>
       <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
       <exercise workspace="0.5in">
          <image>
      <shortdescription>a graph of the line y = 3/4x+2.5</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex5" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 3/4*(x+2)+1       
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.5in">
          <image>
      <shortdescription>a graph of the line y = 5/4x-1</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-95ex6" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -2/3*(x-7)       
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </exercise>
      </sidebyside>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the slope formula to find the slope between following pairs of points.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(1,4), (3,9)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(-3,3), (4,-5)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(-1,-2), (2,5)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(3,4-6), (2,-2)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(0.5,4.1), (2.5,1.9)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\left(\frac{1}{3},\frac{3}{4}\right), -\left(\frac{5}{3},\frac{7}{4}\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
